WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:04.750
Nintendo is back!

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:09.210
Despite a string of dark days, the classic
gaming company has recently come around to

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:13.990
giving die-hard fans what they’ve truly
wanted for years: mobile games like Pokemon

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:18.650
Go, a new entry into the Zelda franchise,
and the great specs that will be packed into

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:20.369
their upcoming NX system.

00:00:20.369 --> 00:00:25.490
They’ve even finally started to reach out
to classic gamers with their upcoming Mini-NES,

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:27.270
packed with 30 vintage games.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.160
It’s a renaissance for the flagging company.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.640
Looking back, it’s not the first time they
avoided total ruination.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:37.910
Here are a few of the biggest mistakes in
Nintendo’s history.

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:38.910
Virtual Boy

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:44.240
The poster child for Nintendo's boneheaded
ideas, Virtual Boy launched in 1995 and was

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.739
completely discontinued within a year.

00:00:46.739 --> 00:00:51.390
To use it, gamers plant their faces into a
stationary viewing port and play games with

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:57.510
an external controller, while red LEDs simulate
a parallax 3D environment within the system.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:03.079
All of this was years after Nintendo had begun
using better 3D tech on games like Star Fox.

00:01:03.079 --> 00:01:07.539
Nintendo was forced to remove the virtual
reality-like head tracking feature before

00:01:07.539 --> 00:01:11.979
the Virtual Boy's release due to health concerns,
which may have made the system's reception

00:01:11.979 --> 00:01:13.170
even worse.

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:17.659
Only 22 Virtual Boy games were released, and
Nintendo seems to maintain their regret to

00:01:17.659 --> 00:01:21.229
this day, mostly because they were twenty
years too early.

00:01:21.229 --> 00:01:22.679
Power Glove

00:01:22.679 --> 00:01:27.560
Even deeper in Nintendo's history is the terrible
Power Glove, a sweaty mess of plastic from

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:31.840
1990 that players strapped onto their arms
and desperately tried to control through a

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:34.829
grand total of two dedicated games.

00:01:34.829 --> 00:01:38.590
Programmable finger motions make the Power
Glove sound like it might be a fun novelty,

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:43.579
but making Mario jump by flashing devil horns
or poking at your arm with your one free hand

00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:47.369
proved too cumbersome of an alternate control
scheme for most games.

00:01:47.369 --> 00:01:51.459
And the two games that specifically utilized
the device just weren't fun enough to make

00:01:51.459 --> 00:01:53.539
the Power Glove a successful peripheral.

00:01:53.539 --> 00:01:56.640
Plus, it made you look like a gigantic nerd.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:57.640
SNES-CD

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:03.169
It’s a story right out of Mega Man or something:
Nintendo built their own arch-nemesis.

00:02:03.169 --> 00:02:07.349
Back in the late ‘80s, Nintendo was already
looking at CD technology to enhance their

00:02:07.349 --> 00:02:11.440
Super Nintendo, and began to work with Sony
on the prospective console.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:15.130
Legend has it that Sony wasn’t previously
interested in entering the video game market,

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:19.520
but after a few years of working with Nintendo
and getting nowhere fast, they repurposed

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:23.260
the work they’d already done and revealed
the “PlayStation” at an electronics show

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:24.860
in 1991.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:28.540
At the same time, Nintendo revealed their
partnership with Philips, which eventually

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:33.569
resulted in the epically awful CD-i system,
and its legendary Zelda games.

00:02:33.569 --> 00:02:36.920
"Gee, it sure is boring around here!"

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:41.379
Sony and Nintendo never made peace, and the
Playstation eventually overtook Nintendo as

00:02:41.379 --> 00:02:43.890
the console of choice for serious gamers.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:48.790
One good thing did result, however: The Secret
of Mana, developed originally for the CD-i,

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:53.390
was ported to the SNES, and remains one of
the best RPGs of all time.

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:54.390
e-Reader

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:58.530
A peripheral for the Game Boy Advance, the
e-Reader could scan tiny bar codes on the

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:03.329
edge of specific trading cards, collect the
data, and either use that information to augment

00:03:03.329 --> 00:03:06.519
an existing game, or load up a classic NES
game.

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:10.980
The device was relatively popular in Japan
and received support for about six years,

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:14.270
but the whole thing tanked in the US after
just two.

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:19.010
The e-Reader often had difficulty reading
codes, and scanning ten cards to run Excitebike

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:23.420
never really caught on, especially when cartridges
that did the exact same thing were readily

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:24.420
available.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.500
Even the inclusion of bar codes on popular
Pokemon trading cards wasn't enough to make

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.840
the e-Reader seem cool.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:31.840
64DD

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:38.409
The worst ever commercial failure for Nintendo
was the 64DD, a disc drive designed to attach

00:03:38.409 --> 00:03:39.870
to the Nintendo 64.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:46.840
The 64DD suffered four years of delays before
finally releasing in 1999, only in Japan.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:51.530
The device promised to be a hub for buying
Nintendo stuff on a proprietary network, a

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:55.560
place to share content with other gamers,
a music listening station, and all manner

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:57.290
of other interactive things.

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:03.730
Ultimately, only 15,000 units sold, because
a 28k modem and magnetic 64MB discs have never

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:06.819
really been enough to keep anything alive.

00:04:06.819 --> 00:04:08.069
Ignoring mobile gaming

00:04:08.069 --> 00:04:13.549
Even though Nintendo and Niantic’s Pokemon
Go broke all kinds of mobile gaming records,

00:04:13.549 --> 00:04:16.970
Nintendo was excruciatingly slow in getting
into the app market.

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:21.650
According to Forbes, Nintendo owns 4000 mobile
gaming patents which they’re now poised

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:27.900
to use, but Nintendo’s former CEO Satoru
Iwata (who passed away in 2015) had previously

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:31.990
repeatedly shot down the idea of Nintendo
entering the mobile market.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:34.810
Engadget collected Iwata’s quotes, which
include,

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:39.280
“If we did this, Nintendo would cease to
be Nintendo.”

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:44.210
Iwata also said that free games just don’t
offer the level of fun that dedicated console

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:48.240
games could, and were pumped out for quantity
and not quality.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:51.930
Nintendo acknowledged that they were missing
out on profits by ignoring the market, but

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:53.580
continued to shun it anyhow.

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:59.120
By 2014, Iwata’s stance began to change,
but Nintendo was already many years behind

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:00.230
other developers.

00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:02.610
The Nintendo Creators Program

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:07.110
Watching people play video games has become
a huge business, but Nintendo is the only

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:11.389
company to become territorial over the use
of their games in derivative media.

00:05:11.389 --> 00:05:16.370
At one point, Nintendo and YouTube divided
all revenue generated by fan-made videos,

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:20.560
but things changed when Nintendo announced
their Nintendo Creators Program.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:25.580
Under the terms of the program, creators actually
get a small percentage of their own ad revenue...but

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:28.370
only under a slew of oppressive terms and
conditions.

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:33.630
Nintendo has to approve the video as representative
of their brand, so all negative reviews are

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:34.630
out.

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:39.120
This means that Nintendo is essentially only
approving free commercials for their products.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:42.060
(Mario singing commercial)

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:46.810
Nintendo also can claim ownership of any video
they approve, and YouTubers can only make

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:48.620
videos about specific games.

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:53.400
It all falls under a very sticky area of “fair
use,” but it completely falls under making

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.280
Nintendo look like an evil final boss.

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:57.280
Wii U

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:01.900
Even though plenty of Wii Us are in use, the
system is still regarded as a huge commercial

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:06.270
failure when compared its main competitors,
not to mention its immediate predecessor,

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:07.270
the Wii.

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:11.550
There are hundreds of small reasons why the
Wii U hasn't done well, but gamers just can't

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:15.680
take the system seriously without a new Zelda
or Metroid game available.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:19.930
There's a weird touchscreen tablet control
scheme dissonance that few games successfully

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:23.840
overcome, and the tablet controller itself
has a pathetic battery life.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:28.050
The only real killer app, Mario Maker, has
a very specific audience.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:33.070
It seems unlikely that the Wii U will actually
take off before the next Nintendo system emerges.

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:34.270
Thanks for watching!

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:37.960
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:41.900
And leave us a comment to let us know which
mistakes you think Nintendo has made...

